1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,980
Welcome to DisputeGuard, a showcase for managing card payment disputes.

2
00:00:03,980 --> 00:00:10,987
This walkthrough follows a sample missing order from the customer statement
through evidence, AI assistance and human review.

3
00:00:10,987 --> 00:00:14,406
Sign in with a showcase persona, or use a registered account.

4
00:00:14,406 --> 00:00:20,012
Customers see their own records; analysts review cases; administrators
manage access and monitoring.

5
00:00:20,012 --> 00:00:24,384
The payments are sample records, with no bank connection or movement of
money.

6
00:00:25,784 --> 00:00:30,016
The dashboard gives you a quick view of the records available to your role.

7
00:00:30,016 --> 00:00:37,745
Use the totals and recent activity to understand your workload, then move
through the sidebar to the statement, transactions or disputes.

8
00:00:37,745 --> 00:00:39,833
The same console adapts to each role.

9
00:00:39,833 --> 00:00:44,121
A customer sees a personal view, while staff can review the wider portfolio.

10
00:00:44,121 --> 00:00:48,296
Use the named sections to follow one payment all the way through its case.

11
00:00:49,696 --> 00:00:53,169
Open Bank statement and select Check for possible disputes.

12
00:00:53,169 --> 00:00:59,467
The application checks configured patterns such as large payments, unusual
amounts and possible duplicates.

13
00:00:59,467 --> 00:01:02,645
These are explainable review flags, produced by rules.

14
00:01:02,645 --> 00:01:06,589
They do not establish fraud and do not automatically create a case.

15
00:01:06,589 --> 00:01:09,061
Compare the payment with your own records.

16
00:01:09,061 --> 00:01:12,416
You can still raise a concern even when no rule flags it.

17
00:01:12,416 --> 00:01:15,712
Search by merchant or payment number to narrow the list.

18
00:01:17,112 --> 00:01:23,464
For this tutorial, we added a clearly labelled sample order worth two
thousand seven hundred and fifty rand.

19
00:01:23,464 --> 00:01:27,934
Choose Review payment, describe what went wrong, then select Submit dispute.

20
00:01:27,934 --> 00:01:30,875
The example explains that an order did not arrive.

21
00:01:30,875 --> 00:01:35,463
The server checks who owns the payment and prevents duplicate active
disputes.

22
00:01:35,463 --> 00:01:39,521
The concern becomes a submitted case that can be tracked in Disputes.

23
00:01:39,521 --> 00:01:42,168
Creating a case does not reverse the payment.

24
00:01:43,568 --> 00:01:50,436
In the case workspace, attach supporting evidence such as a receipt, order
confirmation or merchant correspondence.

25
00:01:50,436 --> 00:01:53,005
This recording uploads a fictional receipt.

26
00:01:53,005 --> 00:01:57,305
The interface accepts PDF, PNG and JPEG files up to five megabytes each.

27
00:01:57,305 --> 00:02:02,322
Files are format checked and linked to the case; malware scanning is not
configured.

28
00:02:02,322 --> 00:02:06,025
Download file lets an authorised user retrieve the attachment.

29
00:02:06,025 --> 00:02:09,608
Evidence remains supporting material for a person to assess.

30
00:02:11,008 --> 00:02:13,745
Ask assistant is the conversational AI feature.

31
00:02:13,745 --> 00:02:17,531
Select the investigation scope, then ask about a payment or case.

32
00:02:17,531 --> 00:02:21,899
Here we ask what happened to the sample order and what evidence to provide.

33
00:02:21,899 --> 00:02:31,102
The server gathers only records visible to the signed in user, checks case
progress and evidence metadata, and asks the local Ollama model for an
explanation.

34
00:02:31,102 --> 00:02:35,586
The answer label identifies a local model response or a rules based
response.

35
00:02:35,586 --> 00:02:38,440
The assistant cannot approve cases or move money.

36
00:02:39,840 --> 00:02:44,719
Switch to the analyst persona, Nomsa Mahlangu, and open the same case from
the queue.

37
00:02:44,719 --> 00:02:51,551
The workspace shows the current state, version, advisory risk, reason code,
required evidence and deadline information.

38
00:02:51,551 --> 00:02:57,291
Choose begin review, followed by begin investigation when the available
evidence supports that step.

39
00:02:57,291 --> 00:03:01,252
Available actions depend on both the user role and the current state.

40
00:03:01,252 --> 00:03:06,648
Each update is checked by the server, including the case version to prevent
conflicting edits.

41
00:03:08,048 --> 00:03:12,079
Run bounded investigation is a separate AI workflow inside the case.

42
00:03:12,079 --> 00:03:17,237
The application assembles the transaction, evidence metadata and advisory
risk signals.

43
00:03:17,237 --> 00:03:19,786
Ollama proposes a read only step or a stop.

44
00:03:19,786 --> 00:03:28,085
Application code validates proposals against three allowed tool names and
enforces a default limit of three iterations and three tool calls.

45
00:03:28,085 --> 00:03:31,997
The visible history records observations and the stopping outcome.

46
00:03:31,997 --> 00:03:36,503
Context is supplied up front, without database browsing or document reading.

47
00:03:36,503 --> 00:03:40,237
In this recording, the model misstates the amount and currency.

48
00:03:40,237 --> 00:03:44,031
The source payment is two thousand seven hundred and fifty rand.

49
00:03:44,031 --> 00:03:45,632
Verify the original record.

50
00:03:47,032 --> 00:03:48,522
An AI summary is advisory.

51
00:03:48,522 --> 00:03:53,793
An authorised analyst or administrator must assess the evidence and choose
the case outcome.

52
00:03:53,793 --> 00:03:59,122
This demonstration approves the tutorial case after review; that changes the
case state only.

53
00:03:59,122 --> 00:04:01,414
No refund or money transfer takes place.

54
00:04:01,414 --> 00:04:07,487
Download case report creates a branded PDF containing case information and
available evidence attachments.

55
00:04:07,487 --> 00:04:12,472
Keep the report with your review record and compare the outcome with the
audit history.

56
00:04:13,872 --> 00:04:19,795
The Investigations section lists saved investigation records with their
source, summary and activity counts.

57
00:04:19,795 --> 00:04:25,444
Use it to revisit what the assistant produced rather than treating a single
answer as a final decision.

58
00:04:25,444 --> 00:04:29,392
A local model label means a model supplied valid output during that run.

59
00:04:29,392 --> 00:04:32,464
A fallback label means deterministic behaviour was used.

60
00:04:32,464 --> 00:04:39,648
Model availability and response quality can change, so always read the
recorded outcome and return to the underlying case evidence.

61
00:04:41,048 --> 00:04:46,316
The Audit log records dispute lifecycle events in order, including the actor
and state changes.

62
00:04:46,316 --> 00:04:53,913
The application uses a SHA two hundred and fifty six hash chain to make
changes to the recorded sequence detectable through verification.

63
00:04:53,913 --> 00:04:57,130
This helps explain how the case reached its current state.

64
00:04:57,130 --> 00:05:02,398
It is an integrity check on the stored log, not proof that every business
decision was correct.

65
00:05:02,398 --> 00:05:06,224
Compare the final outcome with the case timeline and exported report.

66
00:05:07,624 --> 00:05:10,688
Return to the customer view and open Notifications.

67
00:05:10,688 --> 00:05:17,538
Case and evidence activity appear in the inbox, where you can open the
associated record and mark updates as read.

68
00:05:17,538 --> 00:05:19,401
Email preferences are separate.

69
00:05:19,401 --> 00:05:23,908
The interface shows whether email is in preview mode or SMTP delivery mode.

70
00:05:23,908 --> 00:05:29,856
Preview emails remain inside the application; SMTP mode can send to the
saved address when enabled.

71
00:05:29,856 --> 00:05:32,560
No email is sent as part of this walkthrough.

72
00:05:33,960 --> 00:05:36,661
As administrator Mandla Ndlovu, open Monitoring.

73
00:05:36,661 --> 00:05:39,474
Scan once checks the sample portfolio immediately.

74
00:05:39,474 --> 00:05:45,269
Administrators can also start recurring monitoring and choose the interval
and large payment threshold.

75
00:05:45,269 --> 00:05:49,546
Monitoring runs in the server process and pauses when the server is offline.

76
00:05:49,546 --> 00:05:55,904
Flags notify customers and staff for review, without automatically creating
a case or deciding a payment outcome.

77
00:05:55,904 --> 00:06:01,080
These monitoring checks are deterministic rules, separate from the language
model assistant.

78
00:06:02,480 --> 00:06:08,264
People and access is the administrator workspace for customers, staff
invitations and sample payment entry.

79
00:06:08,264 --> 00:06:15,020
New registered customers begin with an empty statement; an administrator can
add showcase payments for the appropriate owner.

80
00:06:15,020 --> 00:06:17,777
Invitations provide the account acceptance journey.

81
00:06:17,777 --> 00:06:22,209
Use the correct role for each person because it controls what they can see
and do.

82
00:06:22,209 --> 00:06:27,560
This recording only inspects the page and does not send invitations or
change anyone else’s access.

83
00:06:28,960 --> 00:06:36,150
The console supports light and dark themes, English, isiZulu and Afrikaans
navigation, plus a command palette for quick access.

84
00:06:36,150 --> 00:06:38,528
The recording finishes in the light theme.

85
00:06:38,528 --> 00:06:46,171
To repeat the tutorial, start with the customer statement, open a concern,
attach evidence, ask the assistant and move to staff review.

86
00:06:46,171 --> 00:06:54,664
The essential pattern stays the same: scoped records, explainable
assistance, server checked actions and a human decision supported by an
audit trail.
